    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greek</a> port near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayster-river">Cayster</a>, founded by tradition c. 1000 BCE and famed for its sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Artemision</a> acted as bank, refuge and pilgrimage centre; its rebuilt temple was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world">Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> passed from Lydian, Persian, Athenian and Hellenistic overlords to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 133 BCE and became the chief city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-roman-province">Roman Asia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A deposit beneath the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis</a> contained some of the oldest coins ever found, small lumps of electrum from around 600 BCE.</p></aside>
    </section>

